{
  "title": "AI answers first, judgment later",
  "content": "Most mentoring junior guides assume a clean environment and a patient timeline. This is the version of the write-up that includes the part that broke. The dashboards look healthy right up until the incident starts.\n\n## The stance: AI-first answers are fine, AI-first judgment is not\n\nThe contrarian take: the problem is not that junior engineers ask an AI assistant before asking you. The problem is that they stop after the first answer. AI assistants are good at producing plausible, syntactically correct, context-free code. They are bad at knowing which of the three plausible answers applies to *your* system, with *your* failure modes, *your* on-call rotation, and *your* regulatory constraints. Mentoring has to shift from being the source of answers to being the source of judgment — the person who asks \"what happens when this times out?\" and \"who gets paged when it does?\"\n\nMost mentoring advice still assumes the junior's first move is to ask a human. That assumption is gone. A junior with a Copilot or ChatGPT tab will get an answer in seconds, and the answer will often compile. If your mentoring model is \"wait for them to ask, then explain,\" you have already lost the first hour of every problem. The real problem this post solves: how to restructure mentoring so that AI-generated answers become the *start* of a review conversation rather than the end of one.\n\n## Why \"just tell them to ask me first\" fails\n\nThe common advice — \"tell juniors to ask a human before asking AI\" — sounds sensible and is almost unenforceable. It also misreads the economics. An AI answer costs the junior ten seconds and zero social capital. Asking a senior costs a context switch, a Slack thread, and the low-grade anxiety of looking slow. You cannot out-compete that with a policy. You can only change what happens *after* the AI answer arrives.\n\nThere is a second failure: the advice assumes the senior's answer is better. Often it is not. For a self-contained algorithm question, a well-prompted model will produce a cleaner explanation than a rushed senior at 4pm on a Friday. The senior's advantage is not raw knowledge; it is knowledge of *this* codebase, *this* team's risk tolerance, and *this* incident history. Mentoring that tries to compete on raw knowledge is competing on the wrong axis.\n\nA third failure is the most damaging: if you forbid AI use, juniors use it anyway and hide it. Then you lose visibility into the wrong answers they are internalizing. The failure mode is not \"junior used AI\"; it is \"junior used AI, got a confident wrong answer, and shipped it because nobody saw the conversation.\"\n\n## The mental model: AI is a fast, confident intern with no context\n\nTreat the assistant as a very fast, very confident intern who has read every public blog post but has never been on call for your service. That framing changes what you ask the junior to do with its output.\n\nThree properties matter:\n\n1. **It is fluent, not grounded.** It will produce code that looks like it belongs in your repo. It has no idea whether your service uses synchronous HTTP or an event bus, whether your database is Postgres or DynamoDB, or whether your team has a rule against adding new dependencies.\n2. **It is confident under uncertainty.** The model does not signal \"I am guessing\" in a way a junior can reliably detect. A junior who has not yet built a mental model of what *correct* looks like in your domain cannot tell the difference between a textbook answer and a production answer.\n3. **It optimizes for the question asked, not the question that should have been asked.** Ask \"how do I retry this HTTP call?\" and you get a retry loop. You do not get \"should this call be retried at all, or is it non-idempotent and retrying will double-charge the customer?\"\n\nThe mentor's job, under this model, is to supply the context the model lacks and to teach the junior to ask for it. That is a different skill from explaining how a retry loop works.\n\n## The shift: from answer-giver to answer-reviewer\n\nThe practical change is to make the AI conversation an artifact that gets reviewed, not a private transaction. Three concrete practices, in increasing order of ceremony:\n\n**1. Ask for the prompt, not just the answer.** When a junior brings you a solution, the first question is \"what did you ask it?\" A prompt like \"write a function to process orders\" produces generic code. A prompt like \"write a Python function that processes orders from an SQS queue, assumes at-least-once delivery, and must be idempotent on order_id\" produces something you can actually review. Teaching juniors to write the second prompt *is* teaching them to think about idempotency, delivery semantics, and failure modes. The prompt is the design document.\n\n**2. Require a \"what I checked\" note.** Before a junior merges AI-assisted code, ask for three lines in the PR description: what the AI gave them, what they changed, and what they are unsure about. The third line is the gold. \"I'm not sure if this handles the case where the queue message is malformed\" is exactly the sentence you want to see, because it tells you where their mental model has a hole. It also trains the habit of locating uncertainty rather than suppressing it.\n\n**3. Review the diff against the prompt, not against the AI's output.** The AI's output is not the source of truth; the requirement is. A useful review question: \"if the AI had never been involved, what would you have written, and why is this different?\" Sometimes the answer is \"it's the same, I just typed it faster.\" Sometimes it's \"I wouldn't have thought to handle the partial-failure case.\" Both are useful signals.\n\n## A decision rule for when to answer vs. when to make them ask the AI\n\nNot every question deserves the same response. A workable rule, stated as a table because the tradeoff is genuinely side-by-side:\n\n| Situation | Who answers | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Self-contained syntax or API question (\"how do I parse this date format in Python?\") | AI | The answer is public, stable, and cheap to verify by running it. Mentoring here wastes senior time and teaches nothing. |\n| Question about *this* codebase's conventions (\"should this go in the service layer or the handler?\") | Human | The AI does not know your conventions. It will guess plausibly. |\n| Question about a tradeoff with production consequences (\"should we retry this call?\") | Human, with AI as input | The AI can enumerate options; only a human knows your idempotency story and your on-call pain. |\n| Question the junior has not tried to answer themselves | Neither — send them back to formulate a prompt | The act of writing the prompt is the learning. Answering directly skips it. |\n| Debugging a specific error message | AI first, human if unresolved | Error messages are well-represented in training data. The AI is often right and fast. |\n\nThe rule of thumb: **if the answer depends on facts that are not in the public corpus, a human must be in the loop.** Your schema, your SLAs, your incident history, your compliance constraints — none of these are in the model's training data, and the model will not tell you it is guessing.\n\n## Where the \"ask a human first\" advice is still right\n\nI am not arguing that juniors should route everything through AI. There are categories where the human-first rule holds, and pretending otherwise is the failure mode on the other side.\n\n**Anything with a security or compliance dimension.** An AI assistant will happily generate code that logs a full request body, including auth tokens, because the prompt did not say not to. A junior who does not yet know what counts as sensitive data cannot evaluate that output. Security review has to be human, and the earlier the junior learns to flag it, the better.\n\n**Anything where the junior cannot yet tell a good answer from a bad one.** This is the core skill gap. A junior with two years of experience can evaluate a retry loop. A junior in their first month cannot evaluate a database migration strategy, because they have never seen one fail. For those questions, the human should answer first and explain *why* the AI's likely answer would have been wrong. That comparison is the lesson.\n\n**Anything where the social cost of asking is the point.** Part of mentoring is teaching juniors that asking a question is not an admission of incompetence. If every question routes through a machine, the junior never builds the muscle of saying \"I don't know this yet\" to a person. That muscle matters on incident calls, in design reviews, and in performance conversations. Protect it deliberately.\n\n## What this looks like in practice: a reviewable prompt\n\nA concrete example makes the shift tangible. Suppose a junior is asked to add retry logic to an outbound HTTP call. The AI-first version of the question is \"how do I retry an HTTP request in Python?\" The mentored version is a prompt that encodes the constraints:\n\n```python\n# The junior writes this prompt, not the AI:\n# \"Write a Python function using the requests library that calls an\n#  external payment API. It must retry on 5xx and connection errors,\n#  but NOT on 4xx (those are client errors and retrying wastes quota).\n#  It must use exponential backoff with jitter, cap total attempts at 3,\n#  and respect a 2-second per-attempt timeout. The call is NOT idempotent\n#  on the server side unless we pass an Idempotency-Key header, so the\n#  function must generate and reuse one across retries. Show the code\n#  and list the assumptions you made.\"\n```\n\nThe prompt itself is the design review. It forces the junior to decide: which errors are retryable, whether the call is idempotent, how long to wait, and what to do about duplicate charges. The AI will produce code; the junior will have already done the thinking. When the code comes back, the mentor's review is not \"does this retry loop work?\" but \"did you get the idempotency key right, and what happens if the third attempt also fails?\" That is a conversation about failure modes, which is the conversation mentoring is supposed to be.\n\nA useful exercise: have the junior paste the prompt and the AI's response into the PR description. Then the reviewer can see exactly what context was given. If the prompt omits the idempotency requirement, the reviewer catches it before the code is written, not after.\n\n## The failure mode nobody warns about: silent wrong answers\n\nThe dangerous case is not the AI answer that is obviously wrong. It is the one that is subtly wrong in a way that passes tests and code review. A retry loop that retries on 4xx will pass a happy-path test and fail in production by burning quota. A database query that looks correct but does not use the index will pass in staging with 100 rows and time out in production with 10 million. A junior who has not yet internalized \"what does this look like at scale?\" cannot catch these, and the AI will not volunteer the concern.\n\nThe mentoring fix is to make the junior responsible for naming the assumption. A simple habit: after every AI-assisted change, the junior writes one sentence in the PR: \"This assumes X.\" If X is \"the payment API is idempotent,\" the reviewer knows exactly where to push. If the junior cannot name an assumption, that is the signal that they do not yet understand the code well enough to ship it.\n\n## A worked example: the retry loop that retries the wrong thing\n\nConsider a typical AI-generated retry function. The junior asks for \"retry with backoff\" and gets something like:\n\n```python\nimport time\nimport random\nimport requests\n\ndef call_with_retry(url, payload, max_attempts=3):\n    for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n        try:\n            resp = requests.post(url, json=payload, timeout=2)\n            resp.raise_for_status()\n            return resp.json()\n        except requests.HTTPError:\n            if attempt == max_attempts - 1:\n                raise\n            time.sleep((2 ** attempt) + random.uniform(0, 1))\n```\n\nThis code is plausible and wrong for most payment APIs. `raise_for_status()` raises on 4xx as well as 5xx, so a 400 (bad request) gets retried three times, wasting quota and delaying the error. There is no idempotency key, so a retry after a timeout can double-charge. And the `timeout=2` is a connect-and-read timeout, not a total deadline, so a slow-drip response can exceed it.\n\nA mentored junior would have written the prompt with those constraints, and the resulting code would look different. But the more important outcome is that the junior now knows *why* the naive version is wrong. That knowledge transfers to the next API they integrate, with or without AI.\n\n## What to do in the next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your team's pull request template — the file is usually `.github/pull_request_template.md` or `.gitlab/merge_request_templates/Default.md` — and add three lines under a heading like \"AI-assisted changes\": what the AI produced, what you changed, and what you are unsure about. Then tell the next junior you pair with that the third line is the one you care about most. That single change turns a private AI conversation into a reviewable artifact, and it costs you one commit.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Should I ban AI assistants for junior engineers?**\nNo. A ban is unenforceable and drives use underground, which is worse than visible use. The better move is to require that AI-assisted changes are labelled and reviewed with the prompt attached, so wrong answers get caught before merge. Bans also waste the genuine speedup on self-contained questions where the AI is reliably correct.\n\n**How do I tell if a junior is over-relying on AI?**\nLook for the absence of named assumptions. A junior who ships AI-assisted code without being able to say what it assumes is over-relying. A junior who writes \"this assumes the queue delivers at least once\" is using the tool well. The signal is in the PR description, not in the code.\n\n**Does using AI stop juniors from learning?**\nOnly if the AI is used to skip the thinking. Used well, it compresses the lookup phase and leaves more time for the judgment phase, which is where the learning actually happens. The risk is that juniors skip both. The mentoring fix is to make the judgment phase visible and reviewed.\n\n**What is the single best question to ask in an AI-assisted code review?**\n\"What did you ask it, and what did you change?\" That question surfaces the prompt (which reveals the junior's mental model), the diff (which reveals what they noticed), and the gap between them. It is more informative than reviewing the final code alone, because it shows the reasoning that produced it.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [PostgreSQL documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/)\n- [Stripe: idempotent requests](https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
